course: dictionary
lesson: 6861
title: Overcharge: asking for too much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are about money, and some are about machines. Overcharge is a word that works in both places. Read the words around it and its meaning becomes clear. «Being charged too much is called being overcharged.» Charged means asked to pay, and too much means more than it should be.
explain: When you overcharge someone, you ask them to pay more money than the correct price. «The shop overcharged us by £2.» Check your change and your receipt, so you can see if you have been overcharged.
explain: In science, to overcharge a battery means to put too much electricity into it. The pattern is the same as in shopping: too much of something. Ask yourself what has been given too much, and the meaning is usually there in the sentence.
fact: Overcharge is a verb, and an overcharge is a noun: the extra money you were asked to pay.
fact: «Overcharged» means asked to pay more than the correct price.
fact: To overcharge a battery means putting too much electricity into it, which can damage it.
fact: The clue is in the word parts: over means too much, and charge means ask for payment or put power in.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: «The shop asked us to pay too much» still makes sense.
exercise: A shop charges Sam £12 for a book that says £10 on the price tag. What has happened to Sam? | *He has been overcharged | He has been paid money | He has been given a free gift | His book is broken
exercise: «The shop overcharged us by £2.» What does overcharged mean here? | *Asked us to pay £2 more than the correct price | Gave us £2 for free | Counted our money twice | Lost our £2
exercise: In science, what does it mean to overcharge a battery? | To make the battery lighter | To take the battery out of the room | To clean the battery with water | *To put too much electricity into it
test: What is an overcharge? | *The extra money you are asked to pay above the correct price | The price of a whole house | A free gift from a shop | A small bag for shopping
test: Which word means putting too much electricity into a battery? | *Overcharge | Discharge | Undercharge | Recharge
exam: Jayden's bill says £15, but the menu says £12. Which word describes what the shop did? | Undercharged | *Overcharged | Counted | Refunded
